{
  "info": {
    "name": "Dex Mock API v2.41.1",
    "description": "Test collection for the mock Dex service. Base URL defaults to http://localhost:8120. In docker-compose, the service is reachable at http://dex-api:8120.\n\nDex is a **federator, not an identity provider**. It has no user database: real identities live behind connectors, and the only thing Dex stores locally is the `local` connector's static passwords. Everything else it knows about a person exists only because a refresh token remembers it -- which is why `/api/v2/refresh/{user_id}` and `/api/v2/offline-sessions` are the closest thing to a user list.\n\nThree consequences the collection walks through: connectors decide what is possible (the password grant works via `local` and `ldap`, is refused by `github`, and a disabled connector is refused outright); the device authorization grant (RFC 8628) is first-class, with `authorization_pending`, `slow_down`, `access_denied` and `expired_token` each demonstrated; and a client may borrow another client's audience only if listed on that client's `trustedPeers`.\n\nThe gRPC API under `/api/v2` keeps Dex's success-with-flag envelopes: creating a client that exists is 200 with `already_exists: true`, not 409, and deleting one that does not is 200 with `not_found: true`, not 404. Those requests are labelled with the flag they assert rather than '(N expected)', because they really are successful calls.\n\nSeed passwords: amelia OrbitDex2026!, jonas OrbitJonas2026!, rohit OrbitRohit2026!, orbit_sync_bot OrbitSyncBot2026!. Requests labelled '(N expected)' assert an error path.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
